Networking Protocols: A Security Deep Dive - CompTIA Network+ Basics In Depth

This is a running reference of the networking fundamentals that keep coming up in security — the stuff from the CompTIA Network+ syllabus that you need a
solid grip on before the security stuff actually makes sense. I did Comptia Network+ a while back, but I found myself wanting to go deeper on the protocols that
actually show up in security work — not just "what port does SMB use" but why it matters when it's being abused. So this is a deeper pass over the networking 
fundamentals. Having a more in depth understanding of networking including protocols are a good "why does this protocol matter" primer if you're
coming at it from the security side. written for anyone coming into security who wants the "oh, that's why this attack works" layer, not just the exam-level 
definitions.

What is an API — Application Programming Interface - The waiter 
You're in a restaurant. You don't walk into the kitchen and grab the coffee yourself. 
You tell the waiter what you want, they take it to the kitchen, and bring back what the kitchen made.

Restaurant = Tech
You (the customer) =	Your app / your code
The waiter	=The API
The kitchen	= The actual server / database / service
The menu = The API's documentation (what you can order)
"Sorry, we don't do that"	An error response

An API (Application Programming Interface) is just a set of rules that lets one program talk to another. 
Your code says "hey, give me the weather in Melbourne" → the API passes that to the weather service → the service sends back {"temp": 22, "condition": "sunny"}.

You never talk to the kitchen directly. You go through the waiter. The API is the waiter.

So what's an "API key" then?
The cafe lets you order, but they need to know who you are so they can write it on your cup and put on your tab(i ordered a latte not too hot).
You give them your name and it's a take away coffee. That's your API key — it's how the service knows which account is making the request, 
and it decides what you're allowed to order(as long as you pay your tab too.
Lose that name (leak your key), and anyone at the restaurant can order food on your tab.

What is IAM = Identity and Access Management. 

It's the system that decides who you are (identity) and what you're allowed to do (access). 
Its like the bouncer at a club: they check your ID (authentication) and then check the guest list to see if you're allowed in the VIP area (authorization). 
