Kerberos and NTLM
NTLM = you prove who you are by answering a math question derived from your password hash, every single time, to every single server you talk to.
Kerberos = you prove who you are once to a trusted middleman, get a time-limited ticket, and then show that ticket to any service. 
Your password never crosses the network again. 

How Kerberos Actually Works (the ticket flow)

Think of it like a hotel key card system:
You check in (log in). The front desk (the KDC — Key Distribution Center, which runs on your domain controller) verifies your ID and gives you a master key card 
(the TGT — Ticket-Granting Ticket).  This card is encrypted, time-limited (default 10 hours), and only the front desk can issue it. You want to use the pool
(access a file server, email, etc.). You don't go back to the front desk with your ID. You just show the master key card at the pool's kiosk 
(the TGS — Ticket-Granting Service) and get a pool-specific key (a service ticket). You swipe the pool key at the pool door. The door checks it, lets you in.
Done. You want to use the gym next. Same thing — show the master card at the gym kiosk, get a gym key, swipe in. You never show your ID again.
The critical property: your password (or its hash) is only used in step 1, locally, to derive the key that encrypts the TGT request. After that,
it's tickets all the way down. No password, no hash, ever crosses the network to a service.

Why NTLM Is Weaker
NTLM	Kerberos
What crosses the network	A hash of your password (the NT hash) used in a challenge-response	An encrypted ticket — no password material at all
Authentication model	Point-to-point: every client↔server pair does its own handshake	Centralised: one trusted third party (KDC) vouches for you
Mutual authentication	No — the client can't verify the server is real	Yes — both sides prove identity to each other
Delegation	No — a server can't say "I'm acting on behalf of this user" to a third server	Yes — the "double-hop" problem is solved natively
Replay resistance	Weak — NTLMv1 is trivially replayable; NTLMv2 is better but still vulnerable to relay	Strong — tickets are time-stamped, service-specific, and single-use
Encryption	MD4 (NTLMv1) / HMAC-MD5 (NTLMv2)	AES-128 / AES-256
Credential exposure	The NT hash is a standing target: steal it once, use it forever (pass-the-hash)	Tickets expire in minutes-to-hours;
a stolen TGT is useless after it lapses

The Attacks That Make the Difference Concrete
Pass-the-hash (NTLM). An attacker who dumps a local SAM database or LSASS memory gets the NT hash. They can now authenticate to any machine that accepts NTLM,
indefinitely, without ever knowing the password. Kerberos doesn't have this problem because the TGT is a one-time encrypted blob tied to a session key — it's not
a reusable password hash.

NTLM relay. An attacker sits on the wire (ARP poisoning, rogue AP — the stuff we discussed earlier), captures the NTLM challenge-response, 
and replays it to a different server. NTLM has no way to bind the response to the specific server that issued the challenge. 
Kerberos tickets are encrypted with the target service's key — a ticket for the file server is useless at the database server.

No mutual authentication (NTLM). In NTLM, the client proves itself to the server, but the server never proves itself to the client. 
An attacker can impersonate the server (a rogue SMB share, a fake RDP endpoint) and harvest the client's NTLM response. 
Kerberos requires the server to prove it holds the correct service key, closing that gap.

Why Kerberos Isn't Perfect
KDC is a single point of trust. If the domain controller is compromised, the attacker can issue valid tickets for anyone (the "Golden Ticket" attack — forge a
TGT using the KRBTGT account's hash). Kerberoasting. Service tickets for accounts with static service keys can be requested and then cracked offline, 
similar to pass-the-hash but targeting service accounts. Overpass-the-hash / AS-REP roasting. Pre-authentication attacks that target accounts without pre-auth 
or with weak passwords. Complexity. Misconfigured SPNs, unconstrained delegation, and trust relationships between forests create lateral-movement paths that
NTLM's simplicity at least makes easier to reason about. But those are Kerberos implementation weaknesses. The protocol itself is strictly stronger than NTLM 
at every layer.

Quick Version
NTLM is "prove your password hash to every server, every time, with no way to verify the server is real."
Kerberos is "prove your password once to a trusted middleman, get a time-limited ticket, and never send password material across the network again.
" That's why it's the default in Active Directory and why Microsoft has been pushing to disable NTLM entirely (it's off by default for new domains in
Windows Server 2025).

