What SMB Is
SMB (Server Message Block) is the protocol that lets one computer access files, printers, and named pipes on another computer over a network.  
When you open \\server\shared in Windows Explorer, that's SMB doing the work. It's an application-layer (Layer 7) protocol, typically running
over TCP port 445 (modern) or TCP port 139 (legacy, via NetBIOS). 

It's a request-response protocol: the client asks, the server answers.  The stateful session tracks open files, locks, and authentication tokens 
for the lifetime of the connection. 

The Connection Flow
Negotiate — Client and server agree on a dialect (SMB 2.0.2 through 3.1.1).
Authenticate — Client proves identity using Kerberos (preferred) or NTLM (fallback). This is the Kerberos/NTLM discussion from earlier, in action.
Tree connect — Client connects to a specific share (a folder, a printer, a named pipe).
File operations — Open, read, write, lock, close.  Each is a request-response pair. SMB includes file locking so two users editing the same file
don't overwrite each other. 
Disconnect — Session closes. 

Versions at a Glance
Version	    Year	            Transport                	Key Security features
SMB 1.0     (CIFS)1992	      TCP 139 (NetBIOS)      	No signing, no encryption, no integrity. Disabled by default on all modern Windows.
SMB 2.0    	2006(Vista/2008)	TCP 445	                Message signing, larger MTU, improved locking.
SMB 2.1    	2009 (7/2008R2)	  TCP 445	                Multi-channel (RDMA), persistent handles.
SMB 3.0	    2012 (2012)      	TCP 445              	  End-to-end encryption (AES-128-CBC), improved signing.
SMB 3.0.2	  2012 R2	          TCP 445	AES-128-GCM,     encryption per-share.
SMB 3.1.1	  2016 (2016)	      TCP 445	AES-256-GCM,     pre-auth integrity, multi-channel over TCP.

The big jump was SMB 2.0 → 3.0: encryption went from "optional signing" to "mandatory-capable end-to-end." SMB 3.1.1 also added pre-auth integrity,
meaning even unauthenticated packets are signed — which kills a whole class of relay and spoofing attacks that SMB 2.x was vulnerable to.

Why SMB Is a Persistent Security Target? SMB is the lateral movement backbone of Windows networks. 
An attacker who compromises one machine and can authenticate to others via SMB can:
          Read and write files across the network
          Execute code on remote machines (via named pipes / ADMIN$ / C$ shares)
          Dump credentials (LSASS access over a remote session)
          Deploy ransomware to every reachable host 
          That's exactly what WannaCry did in 2017 — it exploited EternalBlue (CVE-2017-0144, an NSA-developed exploit for SMBv1) to self-propagate across unpatched
         Windows machines without any user interaction.  ~230,000 machines in 150 countries in days.
          NotPetya used the same exploit to spread through Ukrainian and global corporate networks. 

SMB is the "how does one Windows machine talk to another" protocol — files, printers, remote execution. It's been around since 1992, it's the default lateral
movement path in every Windows network, and its history of vulnerabilities (EternalBlue → WannaCry → SMBGhost) makes it the single most exploited protocol in 
enterprise environments. The defence is the same playbook we've been building all along: patch, segment, enforce strong auth (Kerberos + signing + encryption),
and monitor for anomalies. 
